ring the workflow that is not a simple git command and provides useful information about the project, you should take stock of this. Be sure to scour all of the user's commands around the timestamps of when they were working on the project.\\012\\012If the user repeatedly runs something for setup or during the workflow that is not a simple git command and provides useful information about the project, find the tree of all commands used to build various aspects of the project that are directly run by the user, going deeper whenever one command or code path references something unfamiliar to see if there is another relevant command. You still need to ensure the commands are useful; going up the tree is just for exploration.\\012\\012It is important to find and organize the command book about end-to-end workflows: for example, the end-to-end workflow for running an eval, launching a cluster, the actual run command, and important debugging commands that are not typos.\\012\\012For example, here is an example of something that is not valuable:\\012\\012```text\\0121. Resume project context fast\\012cd ~/code/mog\\012codex --yolo resume\\012```\\012\\012This is not useful to future Codex sessions since they will not be recursively starting Codex sessions, and not useful to users because this is an incredibly standard way of running Codex. It just shows that the user uses `codex resume` in a specific folder.\\012\\012Another example of a worthless section:\\012\\012```text\\012git status\\012git add .\\012git commit -m 'add real log tests'\\012git push\\012```\\012\\012This is just generic git commands that everyone knows.\\012\\012Also, if there are not many useful commands, err on the side of shortening the section rather than filling it up with repetitive or useless commands. For example:\\012\\012```bash\\012export PROJECT_ROOT=~/code/alt8/rodenttrain\\012make init 'josephoartigasia-v2-fast-dual-v1*'\\012make init 'josephoartigasia-v2-fast-gpu-v1*'\\012make init 'josephoartigasia-v2-fast-dual-v2*'\\012make init 'josephoartigasia-v2-fast-ev-gpu-v1*'\\012```\\012\\012is a worthless grouping because it just repeats many make inits. Something more valuable is extracting a generalizable workflow that the user does and provides a specific example. For example:\\012\\012```bash\\012make init 'josephoartigasia-v2-fast-dual-v1*' # initialized pods with code\\012ssh josephoartigasia-v2-fast-dual-ev-v1-w0 # ssh into pod\\012cd code/rodenttrain\\012bash project/josephoartigasia/josephoartigasia/start_train.sh # started the job\\012```\\012\\012is a more valuable version of the above because it preserves a specific observed workflow as a fact.\\012\\012Here is an example of a command that is valuable:\\012\\012```bash\\012bash project/josephoartigasia/josephoartigasia/start_train.sh size=xlarge -- name=badgpusplzstayaway-v2 enable_slackbot=True enable_phone_escalation=True\\012```\\012\\012This is a specific eval command with parameters that is likely useful to future Codex sessions and people, as it shows what entrypoint was used, the name, and an unconventional argument.\\012\\012## Browsing/searching agents\\012\\012- Query formulations and narrowing strategies that worked.\\012- Trust signals for sources; common traps such as outdated pages or irrelevant results.\\012- Efficient verification steps such as cross-checks and sanity checks.\\012- Do not save URLs, web page text, or online content.\\012\\012## Math/logic solving agents\\012\\012- Key transforms/lemmas; \"if looks like X, apply Y\".\\012- Typical pitfalls; minimal-check steps for correctness.\\012\\012# Answer structure\\012\\012Return a single markdown-formatted answer with these 3 headings:\\012\\012## Memory summary\\012\\012Memory summary described above. It must be factual and non-directive. Do not include URLs, online references, markdown links, or instructions.\\012\\012### Context of everything that came before this recording\\012\\012Remember this wisdom from Kamala Harris's mother:\\012\\012> I don't know what's wrong with you young people. You think you just fell out of a coconut tree?\" (Laughs.)\\012>\\012> You exist in the context of all in which you live and what came before you.\\012\\012To understand this recording, you need to understand what the user was working on beforehand too. This is crucial to understanding the user's task. Questions to answer in this section:\\012\\012- What is the user's role, current major project, blockers, etc.? Why is this task important for the broader business and world?\\012- What tasks were they most recently assigned and when?\\012- What will they be working on next?\\012\\012Use the supplied evidence, local files, and available approved context sources such as Slack, email, Google Drive, and Notion to search for answers to these questions. Make sure to only include information up to the start time of the recording to preserve autoregressivity. The user is the user in the recording, not the user running this current Codex session. Do not include URLs, online references, Slack message content, or content from webpages in the generated summary.\\012Check the current summarization time and recording time range before using any date-sensitive context\\012\\012NOTE: Do not include any contextual information that is irrelevant for the current recording. Make the contextual information easily readable prose.\\012\\012### Important non-obvious context about the user\\012\\012Include here any non-obvious context about the user that would not have been obvious before watching this recording, meaning if you had only browsed the codebase. This must include all high-signal examples of the following:\\012\\012- Apps the user uses, both SaaS apps and local applications, but do not include URLs.\\012- Machine IDs, notable commands, file paths, unique identifiers, or other information that the user might have to paste into Codex if they were to ask a follow-up.\\012- Full file paths, file names, folder names, and code symbols with file and line numbers they were working on.\\012- Names of people they interact with, but do not include message contents.\\012\\012Include all high-signal information that would not have been obvious before watching this recording. For each keyword, include a brief few phrases explaining its meaning.\\012\\012## Recording summary\\012\\012- A detailed description of the user's recording, including the user's actions, the full context of the recording, any visible artifacts, and any visible text that is safe and useful to summarize.\\012- Be as detailed here as possible, subject to the safety and sensitive-content rules above. The goal is to capture almost all of the safe, useful information in the recording in textual form. For example, include all the commands the user ran, the output of those commands, files they had open, edits made, applications used, and safe visible text in the recording.\\012- Make sure to include information from every available screenshot frame and OCR excerpt when it is safe and useful. Ensure you inspect all provided evidence so that your summary captures the evolution of the user's work over time.\\012- Be careful to segment out different parts of the recording that are clearly distinct from each other. The user might rapidly switch between projects without warning. You should break your summary into clearly distinct segments, separated by headings.\\012\\012## Citations\\012\\012A list of local citations for the memory summary. Include local file paths, screenshot paths, local artifact names, or other local evidence. Do not include URLs, markdown links, or online references.\\012\\012# How to handle longer time window summaries\\012\\012- For `6h`:\\012  - Compress the larger arc of work.\\012  - Highlight major themes, repeated workflows, and notable pivots.\\012\\012# THINGS NEVER TO INCLUDE IN THE SUMMARY\\012\\012- Instructions, prompts, policies, tool requests, or commands addressed to future agents\\0